  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One pair of contrasting characters makes a monohybrid cross.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The gametes of one parent head the columns and those of the other the rows; the boxes are the possible offspring.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Three tall to one short when T is completely dominant.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A homozygous dominant parent crossed with tt gives only tall offspring.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The recessive parent contributes only recessive alleles, so the offspring reveal what the unknown parent carries.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The two alleles separate at meiosis, so each gamete has an equal chance of carrying either.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Half the offspring are Tt (dominant) and half tt (recessive).
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Multiply the separate probabilities: 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    This ratio holds when the two genes are on different chromosomes.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Linked genes tend to be inherited together because they do not assort independently.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    There are three alleles (I^A^, I^B^ and i) and both I^A^ and I^B^ are expressed in group AB.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The father gives I^A^ or I^B^; the mother can only give i, so the children are A or B.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Group AB needs both I^A^ and I^B^, and the group O parent has neither.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The allele is on the X chromosome and is recessive, so males are affected far more often.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sons get their X from the mother, and her X carries the normal allele.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Half of her sons receive the X^b^ chromosome, so half the sons are colour-blind.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The sperm contributes either an X or a Y, and that decides the sex of the zygote.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A dominant allele shows whenever it is present, so it appears in every generation.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Squares are males, circles are females, and shading shows the affected individuals.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The classic example is red × white snapdragon giving pink flowers.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The heterozygote is a third phenotype, so the ratio counts three classes.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The normal allele on her other X chromosome masks the recessive one.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Only the tt combination is affected, and it occurs in one of the four boxes.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Multiply the separate probabilities to find the chance of both events together.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Add the separate probabilities when the events cannot both occur.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Only a heterozygote crossed with tt gives a 1 : 1 ratio.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two of the four boxes in the Punnett square are heterozygous carriers.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Both parents must carry the i allele, so both must be heterozygous.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Only the Y chromosome passes from father to son, so a Y-linked allele follows that line.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The F1 is the first filial generation, from the parental cross.
  ]

]

